  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The guys arrive at their destination and hesitate before going inside. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что, мы приехали?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Да, мы на месте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нас уже ждут?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Да, они нас ждут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пошли узнаем, как дела.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я немного волнуюсь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ребята, послушайте меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу вам что-то сказать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В последние дни я перегибал палку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я вел себя очень плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Мы понимаем тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо вам за поддержку.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through the exchange around We Arrived line by line and unpack how the Russian works.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что, мы приехали?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well what, we arrived?' The question word for 'what' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'we've arrived'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Да, мы на месте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, we are in place.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'yes' directly with 'place'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нас уже ждут?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are they waiting for us already?' Max turns a statement into a question purely through rising intonation on the word meaning 'already', followed by 'are waiting'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Да, они нас ждут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, they are waiting for us.' Kostya delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'are waiting'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пошли узнаем, как дела.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's go find out how things are.' Max chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'let's go' for the ongoing action, followed by 'will find out' and 'how'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я немного волнуюсь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am a little worried.' Here Kostya anchors the line on the word meaning 'a bit' and builds the thought around 'am nervous'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ребята, послушайте меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Guys, listen to me.' Max uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'listen'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу вам что-то сказать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want to tell you something.' Max chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'want' for the ongoing action, followed by 'tell'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В последние дни я перегибал палку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In the last days I went overboard.' Max puts the word for 'last' together with 'day' and 'overdo' in a crisp, natural spoken turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я вел себя очень плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I behaved very badly.' Max chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'led' for the ongoing action, followed by 'very much' and 'badly'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Мы понимаем тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We understand you.' Kostya delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'to understand'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо вам за поддержку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you for the support.' Max uses the polite plural 'you' alongside 'support', keeping a formal or respectful register. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
